@*
    ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
    PerfProbe — Phase 19.2 Task 3, route "/perf", mount-registry key
    "PerfProbe". The page the click-to-frame measurement tests drive.

    ── THE CONTRACT ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
    ONE click causes EXACTLY ONE frame, and every click's frame carries the
    SAME number of patches (pinned by PerfProbeTests). The handler is a
    synchronous counter increment: no timer, no async work, no
    OnAfterRender, nothing that could add a second render. The measured
    interval is therefore click -> the one frame that shows the new count.

      [0]    BnButton  "Click"      the click target   (id perf-click)
      [1]    BnText    "Count: <n>" the settle signal  (id perf-count)
      [2..51] BnText   50 rows      (ids perf-row-0 .. perf-row-49)

    Row 0 contains the count, so a click changes a FIXED set of nodes: the
    count label and row 0, two ReplaceText patches. Rows 1..49 are constant
    text; they exist so the frame has a realistic node count to lay out.

    The ids are the names the measurement tests use for these nodes. The wire
    has no id or test-tag prop, so they are not emitted: a shell finds a node
    by its position in the tree above. They are exposed as constants so the
    names live in one place.
    ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
*@
<BnColumn>
    <BnButton Label="Click" OnClick="Increment" />
    <BnText Text="@CountText" />
    @for (int i = 0; i < RowCount; i++)
    {
        <BnText Text="@RowText(i)" />
    }
</BnColumn>

@code {
    internal const string ClickId = "perf-click";
    internal const string CountId = "perf-count";
    internal const string RowIdPrefix = "perf-row-";
    internal const int RowCount = 50;

    private int _count;

    private string CountText => $"Count: {_count}";

    private string RowText(int i) => i == 0 ? $"Row 0 count {_count}" : $"Row {i}";

    private void Increment() => _count++;
}
